</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To further evaluate the dynamic performance of the model under continuous traffic flow, Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-C Comparative experiment ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ Biased-Attention Guided Risk Prediction for Safe Decision-Making at Unsignalized Intersections" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> illustrates the trends of various efficiency metrics over simulation time for the proposed model and the Dawei Wang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> baseline. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-C Comparative experiment ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ Biased-Attention Guided Risk Prediction for Safe Decision-Making at Unsignalized Intersections" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, as the number of simulation steps increases (representing the continuous accumulation of traffic volume), the proposed SAC model consistently maintains lower average waiting times and average queue lengths compared to the baseline model. This indicates that our decision framework not only outperforms in overall performance but also exhibits greater stability in response to sustained traffic pressure, effectively preventing the rapid accumulation of congestion.</p>
</div>
